RECORD OF PROCEEDINGS FOR THE GOVERNING BODY
OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE
Office of City Clerk
October 11, 2010
The Governing Body of the City of Cheyenne met in regular session on this date
beginning at 6:00 p.m. in Council Chambers.  Present were: MAYOR - Richard L. Kaysen;
COUNCIL MEMBERS - Ms. Ash, Ms. Broyles, Ms. Case, Mr. Collins, Dr. Rinne (arriving at
6:04 p.m. following the vote on Consent Agenda), Mr. Spiker and Mr. Valdez. Absent: Mr.
Brown and Mr. Pierson.  Also present: Carol Intlekofer, City Clerk; Barb Dorr, City Treasurer,
and Dan White, City Attorney.  The pledge of allegiance was recited.
Consent Agenda. (All agenda items listed with the designation of [CA] are considered to
be routine items by the governing body and will be enacted by one motion.  There will be no
separate discussion on these items unless a member of the governing body so requests and
support by two other members is received.  Any item removed from the Consent Agenda will be
considered in its normal sequence on the agenda.)  Upon request by Mr. Valdez, and pursuant to
support received by Mr. Collins and Ms. Ash, agenda item #15(c) was removed from the
Consent Agenda. Mr. Collins moved to approve remaining Consent Agenda items, seconded by
Mr. Valdez.  Motion carried. Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present.
[CA]
Minutes from Regular Meeting of the Governing Body on September 27, 2010.  Motion
(per Consent Agenda) carried.
[CA]
Minutes from Vouchers Meeting of the Governing Body on October 4, 2010.  Motion
(per Consent Agenda) carried.
Mr. Valdez moved to approve payment of vouchers as presented, seconded by Mr.
Spiker.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present.
“An Ordinance amending Title 17, Zoning, Chapter 17.08, Zoning Districts Established
and Section 17.08.040 Cheyenne city map of the Cheyenne City Code, by changing the zoning
classification from CB Community Business District to HI Heavy Industrial District for Lots 8 &
9, Block 1, Cheyenne Business Parkway, First Filing, Cheyenne, Wyoming (located at the
northeast corner of the intersection of Progress Circle and Campstool Road).”  Reporting for
Public Services Committee, Mr. Collins moved to approve on third and final reading, seconded
by Mr. Spiker.  Ms. Case called a point of order due to a quorum not being present at the Public
Services Committee meeting.  Upon inquiry from Mayor Kaysen, Ms. Case verified the purpose
of her point of order was based on her position that a quorum of the Public Services Committee
was not present at the time the meeting was scheduled to begin, nor when the meeting was called
to order at a later time (City Clerk’s note:  Meeting scheduled to begin at 12:00 p.m. on Oct. 5,
2010 did not convene until 12:16 p.m.), and that three members constitutes a quorum.  Mayor
Kaysen confirmed the Committee meeting was called to order later than the scheduled time and
that three Council members were present, and requested Dan White, City Attorney, provide his
opinion on the issue of whether a quorum was present at the meeting.  Mr. White advised he had
```

### Page 2 of 7

```text
reviewed some standard policies of the City Council adopted in 2002 and that one policy relates
to when a quorum is not present at the time a standing committee of the City Council meets.  He
stated the policy provides that the Council’s executive assistant has the option of contacting
other City Council members and requesting their attendance at the meeting in the absence of a
committee member in order to have a quorum present.  He noted this was the situation that
occurred at the Public Services Committee meeting and that Mr. Spiker was contacted and
requested to attend the meeting at which time Mr. Collins and Dr. Rinne were present.  Mr.
White stated his belief that the policy also provides that the Council member who then attends
the standing committee meeting has the right to vote on any pending matters before the
committee.  He expressed his legal opinion that there was a quorum present at the time the
meeting commenced, however, there was not a quorum at 12:00 p.m., the time the meeting was
scheduled, and advised, under Robert’s Rules of Order, that the Mayor has the right to state
whether the point of order is well taken or not well taken, or the Mayor can allow the City
Council itself to rule on the point of order if the Mayor feels there is doubt regarding the matter.
He further advised if the matter is submitted to a vote of the City Council itself, the question
might be whether the Council rejects the recommendations of the Public Services Committee on
agenda items as reported by Mr. Collins.  In addition, since standing committees are only a
recommending body, even if City Council chooses to reject the recommendations, Mr. White
expressed his belief that the City Council could proceed to consider the agenda matters.  During
further explanation on the point of order that she raised, Ms. Case referenced a previous incident
in 2008 occurring at a standing committee meeting involving a Council member, who was not
assigned as a member of the committee, who requested to be part of the committee and was not
allowed to participate in the committee members’ discussion or vote.  She noted the City
requested and paid for legal research and an opinion on the issue from a local attorney at the
time, and provided Mr. White with a copy of the opinion.  Following review of the opinion and
upon receiving additional information from Ms. Case on circumstances involved in the 2008
incident, Mr. White stated  the previous 2008 situation appeared to be slightly different as the
opinion addresses a former member of Council, who was not an assigned member of the
committee, appearing at the committee meeting and wishing to be involved in Committee
members’ debate and voting, and that the current issue being discussed pertained to a Council
member being contacted and requested to attend a Committee meeting for purposes of a quorum
who then, under the Council’s policy, is allowed to debate and vote.  Further information was
provided by Ms. Case and included recitation of the outside legal counsel’s interpretation of the
phrase “elected official” contained in the opinion and involving City Code Section 2.04.145.
Following comments by other members of City Council relative to standing committees’
composition involving a quorum, committee member assignments and purpose of the 2008
outside legal opinion, Mr. White reiterated his position that the 2008 legal opinion dealt with a
separate issue and his opinion that a quorum was present at the Public Services Committee
meeting in question.  Linda Allard, agent for EchoStar, requested the governing body move
forward with the vote on the zone change ordinance on third reading, whether with or without, if
allowed, a recognized recommendation from the Public Services Committee meeting.  Upon
inquiry as to whether postponement of the item for two weeks would affect EchoStar’s project,
she advised a two week postponement, at this time, would not affect their current plans for
development.  Comments followed on those Public Services Committee members present at the
scheduled time of the meeting, Robert’s Rules of Order and recommendations from a committee,
and reason for the committee meeting starting at a time later than scheduled.  Mayor Kaysen
```

### Page 3 of 7

```text
recognized and reviewed the information that had been presented and discussed relative to the
point of order that had been raised, and ruled the point of order was not well taken and would not
be accepted.  Comments were made in appreciation of EchoStar’s presence in Cheyenne, its
commitment to the community and support for its expansion plans, and zoning uses allowed for
the property pursuant to information contained in the staff report referencing past zoning action
for a proposed use as an early childhood center that has not moved forward in development.
James Perot McNeil spoke in favor of discussing the item at another Public Services Committee
meeting to eliminate any procedural confusion with Mayor Kaysen clarifying the ordinance had
been previously reviewed at two committee and two City Council meetings.  Ms. Allard
requested Council move forward with approval of the ordinance.  Comment followed regarding
the prior zone change from HI to CB and the reason for changing it back to an HI zone.  Motion
carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present. (#3908)
“An Ordinance creating Chapter 17.116.151 of Title 17, Zoning, of the Cheyenne City
Code pertaining to water hauler truck temporary loading sites.” Matt Ashby, Planning Services
Director, reviewed the purpose of the ordinance in conjunction with recent oil exploration work
and staff’s initial belief that timeliness for the ordinance to enact emergency-type regulations
was needed, and stated recently current location options had been made available for water
haulers to access water which has diminished the immediacy to act upon the ordinance.  He
requested the item be withdrawn from the agenda to allow for a hearing process and review and
recommendation by the Planning Commission, indicating following that process the item may be
re-introduced on the agenda.  Dale Steenbergen, Director, Cheyenne Chamber of Commerce,
speaking on behalf of the Chamber and LEADS, expressed concern with the proposed ordinance
section relating to fees to be assessed to water hauler companies.  He requested as the item
moves forward in the process that review be made relative to equitable fees that may be imposed
on water hauler companies vs. other businesses contributing to traffic in the community.  Mayor
Kaysen explained the agenda and meeting process if the item were withdrawn and re-introduced
at a later date following a hearing and recommendation by the Planning Commission.  Reporting
for Finance Committee, Mr. Valdez moved to approve on second reading.  Motion to approve
died due to lack of a second to the motion.
“A Resolution determining that conditions for annexation required by Wyo. Stat. §15-1-
402 exist for the property described as a parcel of land being situated in the SW1/4 of Section
25, T.14N., R.66W., 6 P.M., Laramie County, Wyoming (generally located north of Pershing
th
Boulevard, between Whitney Road and Christensen Road).”  Mr. Collins moved to adopt,
seconded by Mr. Spiker. Ms. Case called a point of order due to a quorum not being present at
the Public Services Committee meeting, explaining the replacement Council person who
participated in the meeting was not a committee member, and requesting the recommendation
from the committee on the agenda item not be accepted and that the governing body move
forward on the item without a committee recommendation. Upon inquiry by Mayor Kaysen, Ms.
Case noted that her comments on the previous point of order she raised earlier in the meeting
also pertained to the current point of order.  Mayor Kaysen ruled the point of order was not well
taken based upon information provided and discussion held on the previous point of order.
Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present.  (#5245)
